    "question": "Hank reads the newspaper every morning, 5 days a week for 30 minutes.  He reads part of a novel every evening, 5 days a week, for 1 hour.  He doubles his reading time on Saturday and Sundays.  How many minutes does Hank spend reading in 1 week?",
    "gold": "810",
    "answer": "He reads 30 minutes in the morning 5 days a week for a total of 30*5 = <<30*5=150>>150 minutes\nHe doubles his reading time on the weekends so 30*2 = <<30*2=60>>60 minutes per day\nHe reads 60 minutes on Saturday and Sunday in the mornings for a total of 60+60 = <<60+60=120>>120 minutes\nHe reads 1 hour in the evenings 5 days a week for a total of 1*5 = <<1*5=5>>5 hours\nHe doubles his reading time on the weekends so 1*2 = <<1*2=2>>2 hours per day\nHe reads 2 hours on Saturday and Sunday in the evenings for a total of 2+2 = <<2+2=4>>4 hours\nHe read 5+4 = 9 hours which, when converted to minutes is 9*60 = 540 minutes\nHe reads 150 minutes in the morning and 120 minutes in the evenings and 540 over the weekend for a total of 150+120+540 = 810 minutes in 1 week\n#### 810"
  },
  "generation": {
    "text": "To determine how many minutes Hank spends reading in one week, we need to break down his reading schedule and calculate the total time spent reading each day of the week.\n\n1. **Daily Reading Schedule:**\n   - **Morning Newspaper Reading:**\n     - Hank reads the newspaper every morning for 30 minutes.\n     - He does this 5 days a week.\n     - Total morning reading time per week: \\( 30 \\text{ minutes/day} \\times 5 \\text{ days} = 150 \\text{ minutes} \\).\n\n   - **Evening Novel Reading:**\n     - Hank reads a novel every evening for 1 hour.\n     - He does this 5 days a week.\n     - Total evening reading time per week: \\( 1 \\text{ hour/day} \\times 5 \\text{ days} = 5 \\text{ hours} \\).\n     - Convert hours to minutes: \\( 5 \\text{ hours} \\times 60 \\text{ minutes/hour} = 300 \\text{ minutes} \\).\n\n2. **Saturday and Sunday Reading:**\n   - On Saturdays and Sundays, Hank doubles his reading time.\n   - **Morning Newspaper Reading:**\n     - He doubles his morning reading time to \\( 30 \\text{ minutes} \\times 2 = 60 \\text{ minutes} \\).\n     - Total morning reading time on Saturdays and Sundays: \\( 60 \\text{ minutes/day} \\times 2 \\text{ days} = 120 \\text{ minutes} \\).\n\n   - **Evening Novel Reading:**\n     - He doubles his evening reading time to \\( 1 \\text{ hour} \\times 2 = 2 \\text{ hours} \\).\n     - Convert hours to minutes: \\( 2 \\text{ hours} \\times 60 \\text{ minutes/hour} = 120 \\text{ minutes} \\).\n     - Total evening reading time on Saturdays and Sundays: \\( 120 \\text{ minutes/day} \\times 2 \\text{ days} = 240 \\text{ minutes} \\).\n\n3. **Total Reading Time in One Week:**\n   - Sum the reading times for all days of the week:\n     \\[\n     150 \\text{ minutes} \\text{ (morning)} + 300 \\text{ minutes} \\text{ (evening)} + 120 \\text{ minutes} \\text{ (morning on Saturday)} + 240 \\text{ minutes} \\text{ (evening on Saturday)} = 810 \\text{ minutes}\n     \\]\n\nThus, the total number of minutes Hank spends reading in one week is \\(\\boxed{810}\\).",
